if the premise is false, but in logical reasoning, we take the given statements as true. Therefore, if the tiger chases the lion, then it must be big. Therefore, the conclusion holds. So the answer should be B) False.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a different interpretation. Let me check the wording of statement 15 again: "If something chases the lion and it visits the dog then it chases the dog." Wait, no, that\'s statement 14. Let me check again.\n\nWait, statement 14 is: "If something chases the lion and it visits the dog then it chases the dog." So that\'s a separate condition. Statement 15 is: "If something chases the lion then it is big." Yes, so that\'s separate. So statement 15 is straightforward: chasing the lion implies being big. Therefore, the tiger must be big. Hence, the answer is B) False.\n\nBut let me check again for any possible contradictions. Suppose the tiger is big, so according to statement 17, it must visit the dog. But the premises don\'t say that. Is there any way the tiger could be big without visiting the dog? The premise 17 says "If something is big then it visits the dog." So that is a material implication: big → visits dog. So if it\'s big, then it must visit the dog. But if it\'s not visiting the dog, then it can\'t be big. Wait, but that\'s the contrapositive. So if we have the tiger is big, then it must visit the dog. But since there\'s no statement that the tiger visits the dog, but there\'s also no statement that it doesn\'t. So perhaps the problem is that we can\'t confirm whether the tiger visits the dog, so maybe the conclusion that it\'s big is not certain?\n\nWait, no. Because the premises must be consistent. Since the tiger is big (from statement 15), then by statement 17, it must visit the dog. Therefore, the conclusion that the tiger visits the dog must be true, even if it\'s not explicitly stated. Therefore, the premises must support that. But the problem didn\'t mention the tiger visiting the dog, so maybe that\'s an 