nd conditional: If something is kind then it chases the tiger.\n\nThe tiger is kind (statement 13). So according to this, since the tiger is kind, then the tiger must chase the tiger? Wait, that can\'t be right. Wait, the phrasing is: "If something is kind then it chases the tiger." So "something" here refers to an entity that is kind. The tiger is kind (statement 13), so the tiger (as the "something") must chase the tiger (itself). But that would mean the tiger chases itself, which might not be possible. However, the question is whether the tiger chases the lion, not itself. Hmm, maybe I misapplied the condition here.\n\nWait, the second conditional says: If [something is kind], then [that something chases the tiger]. So the "something" is the subject here. So if the tiger is kind (statement 13), then the tiger must chase the tiger (the object is "the tiger"). But that would mean the tiger chases itself, which is odd. Alternatively, maybe the object is another entity? Wait, the wording is "it chases the tiger". So "it" refers to the subject (the kind thing). So the tiger (being kind) must chase the tiger (itself). That\'s a bit strange, but perhaps allowed. But in any case, this would not directly lead to the tiger chasing the lion.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the condition is that any kind entity must chase the tiger. So the tiger is kind, so the tiger must chase the tiger. But that\'s a reflexive action. Not sure if that\'s relevant to the question.\n\nMoving on to the third conditional: If something chases the tiger then it visits the dog.\n\nSo if any X chases the tiger, then X visits the dog. \n\nWe know from statement 12 that the tiger chases the dog, but that\'s not chasing the tiger. Let\'s see if any entity is known to chase the tiger. The context says "the tiger chases the dog" (statement 12), so the tiger is chasing the dog, not the other way around. \n\nWait, the lion likes the tiger (statement 6) and visits the tiger (statement 8). But does the lion chase 